WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.180
Some scientists transform medicine.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.880
Some help win wars.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:06.140
John G. Trump did both.

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.280
Born in the Bronx in 1907,

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.440
John G. Trump arrived at MIT in 1931

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.700
to work with Robert Van de Graaff,

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:17.760
then building the world's most powerful generators.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:21.900
Trump's research used vacuum tubes and compressed gas

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.800
to produce high voltage for powerful x-rays,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:28.360
then used for the first radiation treatments for cancer.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:30.360
And then came the war.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.820
Trump helped found the radiation laboratory hosted at MIT,

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:36.980
the center of America's wartime radar effort,

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:41.460
a program of a size and complexity rivaling the Manhattan Project.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:43.840
Trump led the lab's British branch,

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:47.540
and on D-Day, his radars jammed German defenses,

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:51.360
marked drop zones, and guided bombers through cloud cover.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.960
General Karl Spatz called Trump's work

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.540
one of the most important factors contributing to our success.

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:03.540
Peace did not slow him. Trump returned to MIT and co-founded the High Voltage Engineering Corporation,

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:12.320
which brought radiation cancer treatments to hospitals worldwide and became the world's leading supplier of research particle accelerators.

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:24.140
John Trump received the National Medal of Science for his life's work, bringing the widespread beneficial application of ionizing radiation to medicine, industry and atomic physics.

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:28.080
posthumously presented by President Ronald Reagan to his son.

00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:33.480
John Trump devoted his life to pushing his country's science and technology forward.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:36.860
Today, we honor those who do the same.
